        "completedSourceTexts": [
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:1",
            "text": "The price of fashion – economically and environmentally – has led to the rise of a new way of dressing, and it’s beginning to take off in Australia, too. As people now choose to wear more clothes fewer times, clothing rental services have become increasingly popular."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:2",
            "text": "“I think it’s an amazing idea,” says Tanya Perilli, who owns a clothing rental shop. “Customers today look past the fact that something is secondhand and focus instead on the fact that they have something unique to wear and are not overstuffing their own wardrobes（衣柜）or contributing to landfill.”"
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:3",
            "text": "Tanya’s shop offers fashion clothes for women to rent rather than purchase them outright, providing a less expensive solution to one-time event dressing. The concept is certainly not new – men have been renting good suits for decades – but for female shoppers, it is just taking off. This clothing-as-service model follows the broader societal movement towards shared economies."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:grammar-fill:4",
            "text": "Tanya is also looking beyond special-occasion dresses to less formal clothing, which she plans to package as capsule wardrobes and offer to travellers, such as those headed to weddings abroad, with a longer-term rental period. “I really want to make this work for people’s lives today, and I know that doesn’t always mean returning a dress on the Monday after a special weekend,” she says."
          }
        ],
